(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609110700-as-int-is-the-only-i64-divergence-in-the-wave-1-cohort")
  (:adr/topic "q9-wave1-artifact-verified-parity")
  (:adr/title "as-int is the only i64 divergence in the wave-1 cohort, measured against executed artifacts")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110600-the-as-int-divergence-is-a-cohort-decision-not-a-repository-one"
                "adr-2609110500-a-parity-harness-found-the-oracles-own-two-branches-disagree"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

The previous iteration established that all 20 wave-1 pilots carry the same
`as-int` reader conditional, making the choice of a normative branch a cohort
decision. It left one question open: is that the ONLY place a port and its
oracle disagree, or merely the first one found?

It is the only one, in the i64 layers.

## Reproduce

Three pilots have a whole-component build. For each of
`com-abb-robotics`, `com-aadhaar` and `com-aave`:

    # the artifact, executed rather than built
    amu check   src/*/whole_component.kotoba --jvm-free --policy policy.edn
    amu compile src/*/whole_component.kotoba --target aarch64-macos \\
                --policy policy.edn --output a.kexe
    amu extract-native a.kexe --symbol oracle-as-int --output n.bin   # :offset
    KEXE_FUEL=100000000 kexe-loader n.bin <offset> 1 aarch64 7,23 <selector>

    # the oracle
    kbb --backend sci --classpath src:<kotoba-lang/text>/src q9-oracle.cljs

Then compare SELECTOR BY SELECTOR, matching on the label the harness printed
rather than by column position. That detail is not pedantry: the first attempt
printed the two sides as rows of unequal length -- the harness emits seven
`as-int` selectors and the native side had been asked for six -- and the
columns appeared to disagree everywhere. Aligning on the label showed one
disagreement, not six.

## Result

    as-bool      9 of 9 agree, in all three pilots
    page-limit   8 of 8 agree, where measured
    as-int       agrees on every selector except \"12abc\", in all three

`amu check` and `amu compile` returned `:ok true` for all three, and every
number quoted came out of the executed artifact.

## Why this scopes the pending decision rather than adding to it

Naming a normative branch for the reader conditional does not leave a tail of
other mismatches behind it. For these layers it closes the parity question
instead of opening it, so the owner decision that is now blocking wave-1
requalification is a single decision with a bounded consequence.

## The one differing row is what makes the comparison readable

A comparison that reports nothing but agreement cannot be distinguished from a
comparison that never executed the artifact. All three of these runs would have
looked identical -- rows of matching numbers -- had the loader been pointed at
the wrong offset, had the fixtures drifted apart, or had the harness silently
failed to load the oracle.

The `\"12abc\"` row is the thing that rules those out. It differs, it differs in
the same place in all three, and the reason it differs was measured rather than
inferred. Read that way, it is not a defect the cohort has to fix before
trusting the rest; it is the evidence that the rest was actually checked.

## Not claimed

The rendered-JSON hash layers remain uncompared. The `.kotoba` hashes a
rendering, and the rendering is the port's own convention, so a hash
disagreement there would confound a behaviour difference with a layout
difference. Comparing them would need a rendering contract that does not exist
yet, and inventing one to make a number appear would be measuring the harness
rather than the port.

Seventeen of the twenty pilots still have no whole-component build at all, so
nothing here is a statement about them.")))
